{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"01","section":"07","chapter_title":"Digital Citizenship and AI Readiness","title":"Evidence of Digital Citizenship","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting evidence of digital citizenship to digital citizenship evidence portfolio, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the digital citizenship evidence portfolio shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness","cat100_supplement_evidence_of_digital_citizenship","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/01/06"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_calibration"]},"content":"# Evidence of Digital Citizenship\n\n![Evidence of Digital Citizenship textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/01-07-evidence-of-digital-citizenship.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nClaiming a skill and proving a skill are different acts. \"I'm good with technology\" is a claim anyone can make; a captioned screenshot that shows two-factor authentication enabled on your email, mapped to the words \"account safety,\" is proof. This section is about building that proof: a small portfolio where each item is an artifact tied to a specific competency, so a reviewer can verify the skill instead of taking your word for it.\n\nThe work product is a portfolio entry that links one artifact to one learning target with a caption and a short before-after note. A portfolio is not a folder of files; it is an argument that you can do specific things, with the evidence attached to each claim. Twenty unlabeled screenshots prove nothing; one well-captioned, well-mapped artifact proves a skill.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Map a chosen artifact to a specific digital-citizenship competency.\n- Write a caption that states which skill the artifact demonstrates and how.\n- Add a before-after note that shows growth, not just a finished state.\n- Curate: choose the few strongest pieces of evidence rather than collecting everything.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nLin needs to show she learned account safety this module. She has two options. Option one: write \"I got better at digital safety\" in a reflection. Option two: include a screenshot of her two-factor authentication switched on, captioned \"Evidence for account safety: enabled app-based 2FA on my school email so a leaked password alone cannot grant access,\" plus a note that she previously reused one password across three sites.\n\nOption one is a claim; option two is evidence. A reviewer can open the screenshot, read the caption, and confirm the skill without asking Lin to explain. The artifact does the proving.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA portfolio works by the same logic as a resume bullet or a security checklist: a claim is only as strong as the evidence a third party can inspect. A strong portfolio entry has four parts that turn a file into proof.\n\n| Part | Question it answers | Example |\n|------|---------------------|---------|\n| Target | Which competency does this show? | \"Account safety\" |\n| Artifact | What is the evidence? | Screenshot of 2FA enabled |\n| Caption | How does the artifact show the skill? | \"Enabled app-based 2FA on my email\" |\n| Before-after | What changed (growth)? | \"Was reusing one password; now unique + 2FA\" |\n\nThe before-after note is what separates a portfolio from a trophy case. A finished artifact shows a state; a before-after shows learning. Reviewers, employers, and instructors are evaluating growth and capability, so the most valuable entries make the change visible. The second key habit is curation: a portfolio of three sharp, mapped artifacts beats a dump of fifty files, because each strong entry carries an explicit claim while a pile carries none.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A student submits forty unlabeled screenshots and one classmate submits three screenshots, each captioned and mapped to a named competency with a before-after note. Which portfolio proves more skill to a reviewer?\" explanation=\"Volume is not proof. An unlabeled pile forces the reviewer to guess what each file shows, and a guess is not verification. A curated, mapped set lets a stranger confirm each named competency, which is exactly what a portfolio exists to do.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The forty-screenshot portfolio, because more evidence is always more convincing\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The three-entry portfolio, because each artifact is mapped, captioned, and shows growth a reviewer can verify\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"They are equal, since both contain real artifacts\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Neither, because screenshots cannot prove a skill\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe weak version below sounds plausible, but it does not yet give a reader enough evidence:\n\n> I improved my digital citizenship skills.\n\nThat sentence is too thin because it names no competency, no artifact, and no change. A reviewer has nothing to inspect. A strong entry shows the target, the artifact, the caption that links them, and the before-after that proves growth.\n\n### Worked Example: building one portfolio entry, step by step\n\nTurn a finished task into a portfolio entry that proves a digital citizenship skill.\n\n1. **Pick the learning target.** Target: account safety.\n2. **Choose one artifact.** A screenshot of your two-factor authentication turned on for your school email.\n3. **Write the caption that maps it to the target.** `Evidence for \"account safety\": enabled app-based 2FA on my school email so a leaked password alone cannot grant access.`\n4. **Add a short process note.** `Before: same password reused across three sites. After: unique passphrase plus 2FA. Decided after the Privacy section showed how breaches spread.`\n5. **Confirm the claim is backed.** The reflection says \"I improved my account safety,\" and the linked screenshot plus note proves it.\n\nResult: one item, captioned, mapped to a target, with a before/after a reviewer can open. That is stronger than twenty unlabeled screenshots.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Predict each move as you build the account-safety portfolio entry\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You want to prove you learned account safety. Which is the strongest first move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Write \\\"I got better at digital safety\\\" in a reflection\",\"Pick the specific competency the entry will prove: account safety\",\"Upload every file from the module\",\"Add a decorative banner to the slide\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Build claim-first. Naming the exact competency tells you which single artifact will best prove it, instead of collecting files and hoping one fits.\"},{\"prompt\":\"For account safety, which artifact is the most verifiable evidence?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A sentence saying you are careful online\",\"A screenshot of two-factor authentication enabled on your school email\",\"A list of websites you visit\",\"A photo of your laptop\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The 2FA screenshot is inspectable: a reviewer can open it and confirm the setting. A claim of being careful is an assertion with nothing to check.\"},{\"prompt\":\"What turns that screenshot from a file into proof of the skill?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A caption mapping it to the target, such as \\\"Evidence for account safety: enabled app-based 2FA so a leaked password alone cannot grant access\\\"\",\"A bigger image\",\"A timestamp only\",\"Nothing; the screenshot speaks for itself\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"The caption links the artifact to the named competency and explains how it demonstrates the skill, so a stranger can verify it without you present.\"},{\"prompt\":\"What makes the entry show learning rather than just a finished state?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Adding more screenshots\",\"A before-after note: was reusing one password across three sites; now unique passphrase plus 2FA\",\"Removing the caption\",\"Marking it complete\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The before-after note evidences growth, which is what a portfolio is meant to demonstrate. A finished state alone could have been easy all along.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Final entry: target, verifiable artifact, mapping caption, and a before-after note. A stranger can confirm the competency, which beats any pile of unlabeled files.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nTo lock the structure in, order the four moves that build one portfolio entry.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Pick the specific competency the entry will prove\",\"Choose the single most verifiable artifact for that competency\",\"Write a caption mapping the artifact to the target\",\"Add a before-after note that shows what changed\",\"Confirm a stranger could verify the skill from the entry alone\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor every item you consider adding, ask whether it proves a named skill to someone who has never met you. If the artifact, caption, and note together let a stranger verify the competency, it earns a place. If the item is just a screenshot with no claim attached, either give it a target and caption or leave it out. Quantity is not the goal; verifiable claims are.\n\n1. **Mapping check:** Does this artifact tie to a specific competency, not a vague \"skills\"?\n2. **Caption check:** Does the caption explain how the artifact demonstrates the skill?\n3. **Growth check:** Does a before-after note show what changed?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nA common mistake is treating volume as strength: adding every file from the course in the belief that more evidence is more convincing. The opposite is true. An unlabeled pile forces the reviewer to guess what each item proves, and a guess is not proof. A curated set of a few mapped artifacts is read in full and believed; a dump is skimmed and discounted.\n\nA second misreading is showing only finished products with no before. A polished final artifact can be impressive, but it does not show that you learned anything, since it could have been easy for you all along. The before-after note is what demonstrates growth, which is what a portfolio is meant to evidence.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nImagine assembling a four-entry portfolio for Module 1. You map one entry to each major skill: a resume before-after for professional presence, an AI-use disclosure for responsible AI use, a verification log for prompting with verification, and a 2FA screenshot for account safety. For each, you attach the artifact, write a caption naming the competency, and add a before-after note. The disclosure entry, for instance, is captioned \"Evidence for responsible AI use: disclosed brainstorming help and rejected a thesis rewrite,\" with a note that an earlier draft had only said \"I used AI a little.\"\n\nThe walkthrough's lesson is that a portfolio is built claim-first, not file-first. You start from the competencies you want to prove, then select the single best artifact for each. This keeps the portfolio small and every entry load-bearing. A reviewer reading it can confirm four distinct skills in a few minutes, which is exactly the outcome a portfolio exists to produce.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nWhen you review a portfolio (yours or a peer's), read each entry and ask, \"What skill does this prove, and could I confirm it without the author present?\" If an entry has no named target or no caption, it is a file, not evidence. If it shows only a finished state, it proves a state but not learning. Strong entries pass both tests.\n\nA strong reader of a portfolio can answer four questions:\n\n1. Is each artifact mapped to a specific competency?\n2. Does each caption explain how the artifact shows the skill?\n3. Does each entry show growth, not just a finished product?\n4. Is the set curated, or is it an unfiltered dump?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour portfolio entry is strong when a stranger could verify the named competency from the artifact, caption, and note alone. The earlier layers are the target mapping, the artifact choice, and the before-after note; the last layer is presentation. If you are unsure which artifact best proves a skill, mark it in your trace and ask BigAL to help you compare two candidate artifacts against the competency rather than asking it to write the reflection for you.\n\nA useful note would read: \"For account safety I chose the 2FA screenshot over a generic 'I'm careful online' statement, because the screenshot is verifiable. The caption maps it to the competency and the before-after note shows I moved from reused passwords to unique passwords plus 2FA.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the portfolio skill into a job-application setting, where the \"portfolio\" becomes a set of work samples or a LinkedIn featured section. The four-part structure transfers exactly: a hiring manager wants to see a specific competency, an artifact that proves it, a caption explaining your role, and ideally evidence of impact or growth. The constraint shifts because the audience is busier and more skeptical than an instructor, so curation matters even more; you might show three samples, not ten. The principle, evidence over assertion, mapped and captioned, does not change between a class portfolio and a professional one. Learning to build claim-first, verifiable evidence here is what lets you assemble a convincing work portfolio later without starting over.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the digital citizenship evidence portfolio packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/01-07-evidence-of-digital-citizenship-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"digital citizenship evidence portfolio\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"01.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show an unmapped artifact or a vague claim. The revised version should show the artifact mapped to a target with a caption and a before-after note. The trace should explain why the entry now proves the skill.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare an unlabeled screenshot with a mapped, captioned entry.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the four-part entry structure to a job-application sample.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes which artifact you choose.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are building a portfolio entry for CAT 100 01.07. A peer comment says the entry is just a screenshot with no claim attached. Choose the next revision move and the caption and before-after note that would make it prove a named competency.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_evidence_of_digital_citizenship\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_evidence_of_digital_citizenship\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that each portfolio entry maps an artifact to a named competency, includes a caption that explains the link, and shows a before-after that demonstrates growth.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_of_digital_citizenship","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_evidence_of_digital_citizenship","cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness"],"stem":"You add a screenshot of your two-factor setup to your digital citizenship portfolio. What turns it into usable evidence?","options":["Leaving it as just the image, since it speaks for itself.","Adding a caption: \"Enabled 2FA on my school email to protect my account; shows the account safety skill from this module.\"","Adding ten more screenshots so the portfolio looks full.","Renaming the file to portfolio1.png."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Evidence needs context: what it is, which skill it demonstrates, and what you decided. A bare screenshot leaves the reviewer guessing.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_screenshot_without_context","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_of_digital_citizenship","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_evidence_of_digital_citizenship"],"stem":"Your module has four learning targets. Which portfolio is the strongest evidence of digital citizenship?","options":["Twenty loosely related screenshots with no labels.","Four items, each captioned and mapped to one learning target, with a short note on what you decided or revised.","One long reflection essay with no artifacts attached.","The single most visually polished item, repeated in three formats."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A few relevant items that each map to a target show competence better than a large pile of unlabeled files.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_quantity_over_quality","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"You ask AI to write your portfolio reflection. It produces a polished paragraph claiming you \"mastered data privacy.\" Your portfolio has no privacy artifact. What is the responsible move?","options":["Submit the AI reflection; it sounds confident.","Either attach a real privacy artifact that supports the claim or revise the reflection to match what you actually did, then disclose the AI help.","Keep the claim and hope the reviewer does not check.","Delete all reflections so no claims need evidence."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Every skill claim must be backed by an artifact. AI polish cannot substitute for evidence, and AI use should be disclosed.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_claim_without_artifact","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_of_digital_citizenship","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_evidence_of_digital_citizenship","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Your portfolio shows only the final, polished resume. A reviewer wants to see growth. What single addition best demonstrates your digital citizenship process?","options":["A nicer font on the final resume.","A short before/after note: the original duty-only bullet, the revised result-based bullet, and one line on why you changed it.","A second copy of the final resume.","A statement that you worked hard on it."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A before/after with a reason shows the reasoning behind the work, which is what process evidence requires; a polished final file alone does not.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_7_evidence_of_digital_citizenship_no_process_trail","difficulty":"medium"}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"2bf8c22088f47b7ee7c03b397efedca886172615f8274e2357261b62f0b5604b","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}